  q2Focus: 'from "At night the rats came" to "flinching at every sound."',
  q2Text: 'to convey the conditions in the trench?',
  q2Answer45:
    'The rats are personified as having "confidence" and seeming "insolent," which makes them seem like they own the trench. The phrase "this place belonged to them" suggests the soldiers are the intruders. Private Ellis is described as someone who "was afraid of very little" but "was afraid of the rats," showing how extreme the conditions are.',
  // FACT-CHECK 2026-04: source attribution corrected per verified-library audit
  q2Answer67:
    'The writer inverts the expected hierarchy by granting the rats agency while reducing soldiers to passive vulnerability. The rats\' "insolent" confidence personifies them as contemptuous occupiers. The parenthetical aside "which, when you thought about it, was probably true" is devastatingly understated: the shift to second person draws the reader into the narrator\'s bleak logic that the rats, who will outlast the soldiers, are the true inhabitants. The antithesis in Ellis\'s characterisation - "afraid of very little... afraid of the rats" - crystallises the irrational, dehumanising nature of trench life. The present participle "flinching" captures ongoing, involuntary terror.',
