  q3Answer45:
    'The writer starts with physical conditions - mud, water, rain - to establish misery. The second paragraph focuses on a specific soldier to humanise the experience. The third paragraph shifts to nighttime and rats, increasing discomfort. The final paragraph reveals the real source of dread: the morning attack. The structure moves from physical hardship to psychological fear.',
  q3Answer67:
    'The writer structures the extract as a temporal countdown disguised within static description. The opening establishes environment as antagonist. Paragraph two\'s devastating juxtaposition - "nineteen and looked forty" - compresses time structurally. The rats in paragraph three function as a bridge between physical misery and existential dread. The final paragraph is the structural climax yet describes not action but anticipation. The clinical listing of times - "0530... 0600... 0630" - contrasts with the narrator\'s devastating final assertion. The text\'s power lies in what it withholds: the attack remains beyond the extract\'s boundary.',
