const p1_03 = makeP1(3, {
  set: '3',
  source: 'Original contemporary fiction composition (no real author/book by this name)',
  extract: `The changes had come slowly - so slowly that most people did not notice until it was too late to object. First the cameras, mounted on every lamppost and traffic light, their red eyes blinking day and night. Then the screens: vast, luminous panels on the sides of buildings that broadcast government messages in a loop - health advice, productivity targets, reminders to report suspicious behaviour. Citizens were required to carry their registration cards at all times, and public gatherings of more than four people required a permit filed seventy-two hours in advance. And no one complained.

Kessa walked to work each morning along the Avenue of Unity, past the bronze statue of the Director and the flower beds that were always, somehow, immaculate. She lived with her mother in a two-room apartment on the fourteenth floor of Block 9, Sector East. The apartment was clean, adequately heated, and exactly the same as every other apartment in the block. There was nothing wrong with it. That was precisely the problem.

At the Bureau of Information, where Kessa worked as a junior data clerk, the day began with the Affirmation - a two-minute recording played over the loudspeakers in which a calm, pleasant voice listed the values of the State: order, efficiency, collective purpose, gratitude. Kessa mouthed the words without thinking. Everyone did. It was not that she believed them, exactly. It was that the question of belief had become irrelevant. You said the words. You did your work. You went home. The machine continued.

But lately something had shifted. A feeling she could not name - like a stone lodged beneath her ribs, small and hard and impossible to ignore. She had begun to notice things: the way her colleague Dara flinched when the supervisor passed; the empty desk in the corner that no one mentioned; the slight tremor in her mother's hands when the evening broadcast began.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'up to "no one complained."',
  q1Options:
    'A) The city had changed gradually.\nB) The screens displayed advertisements.\nC) Citizens carried identification at all times.\nD) The narrator lived alone.\nE) Public gatherings were monitored.\nF) The narrator felt safe.\nG) The changes had happened over several years.\nH) People had accepted the changes without protest.',
  q1Answers45:
    'A, C, E, H - A: "The changes had come slowly." C: "required to carry their registration cards at all times." E: "public gatherings of more than four people required a permit." H: "no one complained."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, C, E, H - B is false (screens show government messages). D is false (she lives with her mother). F is false (unease pervades). G is not explicitly stated - "slowly" does not confirm "years."',
  q2Focus: 'from the third paragraph, "At the Bureau of Information" to "The machine continued."',
  q2Text: 'to present the effects of state control on individuals?',
  q2Answer45:
    'The writer lists "order, efficiency, collective purpose, gratitude" to show the State\'s values, which sound positive but feel controlling. The short sentences "You said the words. You did your work. You went home." create a robotic rhythm. The metaphor "The machine continued" compares the state to a machine that keeps going regardless of people.',
  // FACT-CHECK 2026-04: source attribution corrected per verified-library audit
  q2Answer67:
    'The writer uses the Affirmation as a microcosm of totalitarian control. The "calm, pleasant voice" is more sinister than overt threat - its pleasantness masks compulsion. The listed values form an ascending scale of submission. The verb "mouthed" denotes physical speech without interior meaning, capturing hollow compliance. The staccato sequence "You said the words. You did your work. You went home." shifts to second person, universalising Kessa\'s experience into an instruction manual for survival. The metaphor "The machine continued" reduces the State to a self-perpetuating mechanism.',
  q3Text:
    "How has the writer structured the text to develop the reader's understanding of this world and Kessa's growing awareness?",
  q3Answer45:
    'The extract starts with the whole city\'s surveillance, then narrows to Kessa\'s routine, then to her workplace, and finally to her inner feelings. This narrowing takes the reader from understanding the system to understanding one person within it. The word "But" at the start of the final paragraph signals change - Kessa is beginning to question things.',
  q3Answer67:
    'The writer structures the extract as a progressive narrowing from systemic to personal. Paragraph one establishes macro-level control using documentary register. Paragraph two narrows to Kessa\'s geography, the specificity of "Block 9, Sector East" implying a world catalogued into grids. Paragraph three moves inward to psychology, where staccato sentences perform the automation of inner life. The structural volta arrives with "But" opening paragraph four. The final tricolon of observed details - the flinch, the empty desk, the tremor - functions as evidence of awakening: Kessa seeing what the previous paragraphs trained her not to see.',
  q4Focus: 'from "At the Bureau of Information."',
  q4Statement:
    'The writer creates a world that is frightening not because of violence, but because of the absence of any resistance. The quiet acceptance is the most disturbing thing.',
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I agree that the quiet acceptance is disturbing. Kessa "mouthed the words without thinking" and "everyone did." The statement "the question of belief had become irrelevant" is frightening because people have stopped thinking about right and wrong. The empty desk implies someone has disappeared and nobody dares ask. However, the ending suggests Kessa is beginning to resist - the "stone lodged beneath her ribs" shows she cannot ignore her doubts.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I agree substantially. The writer crafts a world where absence of overt violence makes control more insidious. The Affirmation requires no force because compliance has become reflexive. The most chilling sentence is "the question of belief had become irrelevant" - suggesting not suppression of dissent but its obsolescence. The "empty desk in the corner that no one mentioned" implies disappearance; the silence around it is collective complicity. However, the final paragraph suggests resistance has been driven inward rather than eradicated. The simile of the stone "lodged beneath her ribs" locates dissent in the body, beneath consciousness. The text is disturbing not because resistance is absent but because it has been reduced to a nameless physical sensation.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    'Imagine a photograph of an empty city street at dawn, with identical buildings stretching into the distance and a single figure walking away from the camera.',
  q5NarrativePrompt:
    'Write the opening part of a story about a character who begins to question something everyone else accepts.',
})

const p1_04 = makeP1(4, {
  set: '4',
  source: 'Original contemporary fiction composition (no real author/book by this name)',
  extract: `I was fourteen the summer we left Ashford Road, and I understood, in the way that children understand difficult things - not with the mind but with the stomach - that nothing afterwards would be the same. The removal van came at seven in the morning, two men in grey overalls who handled our furniture with a cheerful indifference that seemed almost offensive. Our sofa, our kitchen table, the bookshelf my father had built from reclaimed wood - they carried it all out as though it were anyone's.

My bedroom was the last room to be emptied. I stood in the doorway and looked at the space where my bed had been - a rectangle of darker carpet, like a shadow of something that had gone. The walls still held the marks of Blu-Tack where my posters had hung. Four small blue circles, evenly spaced. Evidence of a life.

Outside, the August heat pressed down on everything. The apple tree at the end of the garden - the one I had climbed every summer since I was six - stood motionless in the still air, its leaves dark and heavy. My mother was in the front seat of the car, sunglasses on, looking straight ahead. My father loaded the last box into the boot and said, "Right then." I did not say goodbye.

We drove in silence through streets I had known my entire life. The corner shop where Mr Abbasi gave us free sweets on Fridays. The park where I had learned to ride a bike, falling and falling and falling until I didn't. The low wall outside number thirty-seven where Priya and I had sat every evening for three summers, talking about everything and nothing, our legs swinging, the whole world ahead of us and none of it real yet. I pressed my face against the car window and watched it all slide away, and I felt something close inside me - quietly, permanently - like a book being shut.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'up to "I did not say goodbye."',
  q1Options:
    "A) The narrator was fourteen years old.\nB) The family moved in the winter.\nC) The removal van arrived in the morning.\nD) The narrator's bedroom was the first room to be emptied.\nE) The garden had an apple tree.\nF) The narrator's mother was loading the car.\nG) The weather was hot.\nH) The narrator said goodbye to the house.",
  q1Answers45:
    'A, C, E, G - A: "I was fourteen." C: "came at seven in the morning." E: "The apple tree at the end of the garden." G: "the August heat pressed down."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, C, E, G - B is false: it was "the summer we left Ashford". D is false: "My bedroom was the last room to be emptied." F is false: "My mother was in the front seat" while "My father loaded the last box". H is false: "I did not say goodbye."',
  q2Focus: 'from the final paragraph, "We drove in silence."',
  q2Text: "to convey the narrator's sense of loss?",
  q2Answer45:
    'The writer lists familiar places - the corner shop, the park, the wall - to show everything being lost. The repetition of "falling and falling and falling" emphasises the memory vividly. The simile "like a book being shut" at the end suggests a chapter of life closing permanently. The phrase "everything and nothing" shows how conversations with Priya felt both important and casual.',
  // FACT-CHECK 2026-04: source attribution corrected per verified-library audit
  q2Answer67:
    'The writer constructs the paragraph as a catalogue of loss through accumulated specificity. The sentence fragments - "The corner shop where Mr Abbasi gave us free sweets on Fridays" - function as a litany, each memory a discrete unit of grief. Proper nouns (Mr Abbasi, Priya, number thirty-seven) resist generalisation; these are irreplaceable particularities. The triple repetition "falling and falling and falling until I didn\'t" uses polysyndeton to stretch time, while the abrupt "until I didn\'t" collapses process into achievement. The final simile - "like a book being shut" - is devastating: "shut" rather than "slammed" implies not violence but finality, while "quietly, permanently" pairs gentleness with irreversibility.',
  q3Text: 'How has the writer structured the text to convey the experience of leaving?',
  q3Answer45:
    "The text moves from inside the house to outside and then through the streets, mirroring the process of leaving. Each paragraph takes the narrator further from home. The opening tells us the outcome - they are leaving - creating inevitability. The ending's simile provides emotional closure matching the physical departure.",
  q3Answer67:
    'The writer structures the extract as simultaneous physical and emotional departure. The opening establishes retrospective knowledge - "nothing afterwards would be the same" - colouring every detail with significance. Spatial movement is progressive: bedroom, garden, car, street. The second paragraph\'s focus on absence (the "rectangle of darker carpet," Blu-Tack marks) structurally enacts loss before departure is complete. The father\'s terse "Right then" marks the point of no return. The final paragraph accelerates through accumulated memories before the closing image arrests motion, the book metaphor providing structural closure mirroring emotional closure.',
  q4Focus: 'from "Outside, the August heat."',
  q4Statement:
    "This writing is so effective because the narrator doesn't dramatise her feelings - the pain is in the small, ordinary details.",
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I agree that small details make this effective. The apple tree she "climbed every summer since I was six" represents years of childhood. The father saying "Right then" is a simple phrase meaning they are leaving forever. The narrator does not cry or shout - she just watches things "slide away." This restraint makes the emotion feel real.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I agree strongly. The writer\'s technique is emotional displacement - grief is encoded in observed particulars rather than stated directly. The mother\'s "sunglasses on, looking straight ahead" functions as a shield against emotion; the forward gaze is a refusal to look back. The father\'s "Right then" is devastatingly mundane - two words compressing displacement into everyday speech. The catalogue of places works because each memory is specific and sensory: "free sweets on Fridays," "legs swinging." These are habitual memories, and their ordinariness makes their loss affecting. However, the final simile - "like a book being shut" - moves beyond the purely ordinary into conscious literary metaphor, suggesting the narrator is shaping the experience in retrospect.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    "Imagine a photograph of a child's empty bedroom with boxes stacked against one wall and sunlight streaming through a bare window.",
  q5NarrativePrompt: 'Write the opening part of a story about a last day.',
})

const p1_05 = makeP1(5, {
  set: '5',
  source: 'Original contemporary fiction composition (no real author/book by this name)',
  extract: `Sarah had been alone in the house for three hours when she first noticed the change. It was nothing she could identify precisely - not a sound, not a movement - but a shift in the quality of the silence, as though the air itself had thickened. She set down her book and listened. The clock on the mantelpiece ticked. The refrigerator hummed in the kitchen. Outside, a car passed on the wet road, its tyres hissing. Normal sounds. And yet.

She stood and walked to the hallway. The front door was locked - the deadbolt turned, the chain fastened - though she could not remember locking it.

The stairs rose into darkness. Tom had left for Manchester that morning, kissing her forehead in the half-light of six a.m., and would not be back until Thursday. Two more nights. The house was a Victorian terrace on a quiet street in Didsbury, and she had lived here for four years without incident. There was no reason to feel afraid. She told herself this firmly, the way one does when the telling is necessary.

She climbed the stairs. The bedroom door was open - she was certain she had closed it. The room beyond was dark except for the orange glow of the streetlamp filtering through the curtains, and in that glow she saw - or thought she saw - that the wardrobe door was slightly ajar. She had not opened the wardrobe today. She was almost certain she had not opened the wardrobe today.

The word "almost" settled in her chest like a cold hand.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'up to "she could not remember locking it."',
  q1Options:
    "A) The narrator was alone in the house.\nB) It was evening.\nC) The front door was locked.\nD) The narrator had been reading.\nE) There was a noise from upstairs.\nF) The narrator's phone was on the table.\nG) The house was in a city.\nH) The narrator felt uneasy.",
  q1Answers45:
    'A, C, D, H - A: "Sarah had been alone in the house for three hours." C: "The front door was locked." D: "She set down her book." H: The unease is conveyed throughout - the air "thickened," "And yet."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, C, D, H - B is implied (darkness, streetlamp) but the first section does not confirm it explicitly. E is false in the specified section. F is not stated. G appears after the cut-off (Didsbury detail is in paragraph three).',
  q2Focus: 'from "She climbed the stairs" to the end.',
  q2Text: 'to create suspense and psychological tension?',
  q2Answer45:
    'The writer creates suspense by the bedroom door being open when Sarah "was certain she had closed it." The phrase "or thought she saw" makes us doubt what is real. The repetition - "She had not opened the wardrobe today. She was almost certain" - shows Sarah losing confidence. The final metaphor of "almost" settling "like a cold hand" makes doubt feel physical.',
  // FACT-CHECK 2026-04: source attribution corrected per verified-library audit
  q2Answer67:
    'The writer generates psychological tension through erosion of certainty. The parenthetical "or thought she saw" destabilises the narrative, introducing unreliable perception. The wardrobe repetition is structurally crucial: "She had not opened the wardrobe today" is declarative; "She was almost certain she had not opened the wardrobe today" replays the thought with the devastating qualifier "almost," dismantling the preceding assertion. The final sentence elevates "almost" from adverb to antagonist - personifying the word as a physical presence that "settled in her chest like a cold hand." The simile transforms linguistic doubt into bodily sensation, suggesting uncertainty has become the true threat.',
  q3Text: 'How has the writer structured the text to create and maintain suspense?',
  q3Answer45:
    'The writer starts with normal domestic sounds - clock ticking, fridge humming - before the fragment "And yet" disrupts the calm. Focus moves from downstairs to hallway to stairs to bedroom, each stage adding evidence that something is wrong. The final short paragraph provides a dramatic, isolated ending.',
  q3Answer67:
    'The writer structures the extract as controlled escalation through progressive disclosure of anomalies. The opening establishes normalcy - domestic sounds catalogued reassuringly - before "And yet" fractures the calm as a structural hinge. The second paragraph introduces the first anomaly (the locked door) embedded in ambiguity. The third paragraph performs structural delay: exposition about Tom and the house interrupts investigation, building tension through deferral. The fourth paragraph delivers the most disturbing anomaly, and the repetition with variation - certainty eroding to "almost" - enacts psychological unravelling structurally. The isolated final sentence forces the reader to pause on "almost," granting it disproportionate structural weight.',
  q4Focus: 'from "The stairs rose into darkness."',
  q4Statement:
    "What makes this extract so unsettling is that nothing actually happens - the fear is entirely in the character's mind, and the writer makes us share it.",
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I mostly agree. Nothing violent happens - Sarah just walks upstairs and looks at a wardrobe. But small details make it terrifying: the door she did not leave open, the wardrobe "slightly ajar." The phrase "she told herself this firmly" shows Sarah trying to control her fear. However, the open door and ajar wardrobe suggest something has happened - so the fear may not be entirely in her mind.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I largely agree, though I would complicate the claim that fear is "entirely" psychological. The writer operates in the gap between event and interpretation - the locked door, open bedroom, ajar wardrobe are potentially explicable but collectively resist innocent explanation. The writer forces the reader into Sarah\'s interpretive dilemma: to rationalise each anomaly or accept the terrifying pattern. "The way one does when the telling is necessary" universalises self-deception, implicating the reader. The final image of "almost" as "a cold hand" suggests the real threat is epistemic - not an intruder but the collapse of certainty. The extract is unsettling precisely because we cannot determine whether nothing has happened or something has not yet revealed itself.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    'Imagine a photograph of a dimly lit hallway in a residential house, with a single door slightly ajar at the far end.',
  q5NarrativePrompt:
    'Write the opening part of a story about a character who suspects they are not alone.',
})

const p1_06 = makeP1(6, {
  set: '6',
  source: 'George Eliot, Middlemarch (1871-72), Chapter 20',
  extract: `Two hours later, Dorothea was seated in an inner room or boudoir of a handsome apartment in the Via Sistina.

I am sorry to add that she was sobbing bitterly, with such abandonment to this relief of an oppressed heart as a woman habitually controlled by pride on her own account and thoughtfulness for others will sometimes allow herself when she feels securely alone. And Mr. Casaubon was certain to remain away for some time at the Vatican.

Yet Dorothea had no distinctly shapen grievance that she could state even to herself; and in the midst of her confused thought and passion, the mental act that was struggling forth into clearness was a self-accusing cry that her feeling of desolation was the fault of her own spiritual poverty. She had married the man of her choice, and with the advantage over most girls that she had contemplated her marriage chiefly as the beginning of new duties: from the very first she had thought of Mr. Casaubon as having a mind so much above her own, that he must often be claimed by studies which she could not entirely share; moreover, after the brief narrow experience of her girlhood she was beholding Rome, the city of visible history, where the past of a whole hemisphere seems moving in funeral procession with strange ancestral images and trophies gathered from afar.

But this stupendous fragmentariness heightened the dreamlike strangeness of her bridal life. Dorothea had now been five weeks in Rome, and in the kindly mornings when autumn and winter seemed to go hand in hand like a happy aged couple one of whom would presently survive in chiller loneliness, she had driven about at first with Mr. Casaubon, but of late chiefly with Tantripp and their experienced courier. She had been led through the best galleries, had been taken to the chief points of view, had been shown the grandest ruins and the most glorious churches, and she had ended by oftenest choosing to drive out to the Campagna where she could feel alone with the earth and sky, away-from the oppressive masquerade of ages, in which her own life too seemed to become a masque with enigmatical costumes.`,
  q1TrueStatements:
    'from the beginning to "strange ancestral images and trophies gathered from afar."',
  q1Options:
    "A) Dorothea was in an apartment in the Via Sistina.\nB) Dorothea was crying.\nC) Mr Casaubon was in the room with her.\nD) Dorothea blamed herself for her unhappiness.\nE) Dorothea had been forced into her marriage.\nF) Dorothea could clearly explain what was wrong.\nG) Dorothea had expected her husband's studies to take up his time.\nH) Dorothea had travelled widely before she married.",
  q1Answers45:
    'A, B, D, G - A: "a handsome apartment in the Via Sistina." B: "she was sobbing bitterly." D: "a self-accusing cry that her feeling of desolation was the fault of her own spiritual poverty." G: "he must often be claimed by studies which she could not entirely share."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, B, D, G - C is false: Mr. Casaubon "was certain to remain away for some time at the Vatican." E is false: "She had married the man of her choice." F is false: she "had no distinctly shapen grievance that she could state even to herself." H is false: her girlhood was a "brief narrow experience."',
  q2Focus: 'from "But this stupendous fragmentariness" to the end.',
  q2Text: "to present Dorothea's experience of Rome and of her new marriage?",
  q2Answer45:
    'The writer describes Dorothea\'s married life as having a "dreamlike strangeness", which suggests it does not feel real to her. The simile of autumn and winter going "hand in hand like a happy aged couple" seems gentle, but one of the couple "would presently survive in chiller loneliness", which hints at how alone Dorothea is. The list of what she was shown - "the best galleries", "the grandest ruins", "the most glorious churches" - makes the sightseeing sound tiring rather than exciting. At the end she chooses the Campagna, "where she could feel alone with the earth and sky", which shows that she wants to escape.',
  q2Answer67:
    'Eliot makes Rome a mirror of Dorothea\'s inner state. The phrase "stupendous fragmentariness" presents the city as vast but broken, and the verb "heightened" makes that brokenness intensify "the dreamlike strangeness of her bridal life": the honeymoon feels unreal rather than joyful. The simile of autumn and winter going "hand in hand like a happy aged couple" seems tender until its final clause, in which one partner "would presently survive in chiller loneliness", quietly foreshadowing a marriage that will end in isolation. The shift from driving "at first with Mr. Casaubon" to driving "of late chiefly with Tantripp and their experienced courier" charts Casaubon\'s withdrawal in a single sentence. The passive verbs "had been led", "had been taken" and "had been shown" make Dorothea a spectator of her own life, and the superlatives ("best", "grandest", "most glorious") pile up without pleasure. The paragraph ends with "the oppressive masquerade of ages", in which "her own life too seemed to become a masque with enigmatical costumes": the language of performance suggests that Dorothea no longer understands the part she is playing.',
  q3Text: "How has the writer structured the text to reveal Dorothea's situation to the reader?",
  q3Answer45:
    'The extract begins with a precise time and place - "Two hours later", in an apartment in Rome - and then reveals that Dorothea is "sobbing bitterly", which is a shock. The narrator explains that Mr. Casaubon is away "at the Vatican", so we know she is alone. The third paragraph moves inside Dorothea\'s mind and shows that she blames herself. The final paragraph widens out to her "five weeks in Rome" and ends with her driving out to the Campagna to be alone, which shows how lonely the marriage has become.',
  q3Answer67:
    'Eliot structures the extract as a movement from a single hidden moment to a pattern of weeks. It opens in close focus on "an inner room or boudoir", the most private space in the apartment, and the narrator\'s apologetic "I am sorry to add" delays the revelation that Dorothea is "sobbing bitterly", so that the reader discovers her grief almost as an intrusion. The short sentence placing Mr. Casaubon "at the Vatican" explains her solitude without comment. The third paragraph turns inward: the reader follows Dorothea "in the midst of her confused thought and passion" as she tries to name her unhappiness and ends by blaming "her own spiritual poverty". The final paragraph then pulls back in time to summarise "five weeks in Rome", replacing the single scene with repeated habit. The extract ends in open country, where Dorothea "could feel alone with the earth and sky": it moves from an inner room to the widest space available, yet each step takes her further from her husband.',
  q4Focus: 'from "She had married the man of her choice" to the end.',
  q4Statement:
    'The writer creates deep sympathy for Dorothea by showing how lonely she is - not because anyone is cruel to her, but because of the life she has chosen.',
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I agree. Nobody in the extract is cruel to Dorothea, but she is left alone while her husband studies. The phrase "the man of her choice" reminds us that she chose this marriage, which makes it sadder. The description of her being "led" and "shown" around Rome makes her seem like a visitor in her own life. The ending, where she drives out to the Campagna "where she could feel alone with the earth and sky", shows that she is so lonely she would rather be completely by herself.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I largely agree, and Eliot\'s refusal to give Dorothea a villain is what makes the sympathy deep. The clause "She had married the man of her choice" presents the marriage as freely chosen, and Eliot adds that she had thought of Mr. Casaubon "as having a mind so much above her own" that he "must often be claimed by studies which she could not entirely share": Dorothea expected to be left out, so she cannot even call her loneliness unjust. Rome, "the city of visible history", should fill that gap, but its "stupendous fragmentariness" only deepens "the dreamlike strangeness of her bridal life". The quiet shift from driving "at first with Mr. Casaubon" to driving "of late chiefly with Tantripp" records a marriage emptying out without a single cruel act. I would add one qualification: the loneliness is not only chosen. The simile of the "happy aged couple", one of whom "would presently survive in chiller loneliness", suggests something closer to fate, as if isolation were the natural end of this union. That Dorothea ends up choosing the Campagna, "where she could feel alone with the earth and sky", is the saddest detail of all: being alone in nature has become easier than being alone in company.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    'Imagine a photograph of a woman standing at a tall window, looking out at the rooftops of an old European city in winter light.',
  q5NarrativePrompt:
    'Write the opening part of a story about a character who realises they have made the wrong decision.',
})

const p1_07 = makeP1(7, {
  set: '7',
  source: 'Original contemporary fiction composition (no real author/book by this name)',
  extract: `The boat was not a boat. It was a thing made of wood and prayer, held together by nails that were already rusting and the desperate hope of forty-three people who had paid everything they had to sit in it. It rode low in the water, so low that the Mediterranean lapped at the gunwales like a tongue tasting the edge of a plate, and every wave - even the gentlest - sent a shudder through the hull that you felt in your teeth.

Amara sat near the stern with her daughter pressed against her chest. The child was three years old and had not cried since they left the coast. This silence frightened Amara more than the sea. A child who cries is a child who believes someone will come. A child who is silent has already learned that no one will.

The sky was enormous. That was the thing nobody told you about the open sea - how vast the sky became when there was no land to interrupt it, how it pressed down on you with the weight of all that emptiness. The sun moved across it slowly, indifferently, marking time that meant nothing out here where there were no hours, no appointments, no schedules - only the distance between where you were and where you needed to be, measured not in miles but in the fuel remaining in the single outboard motor that coughed and spluttered like a sick animal.

By the second night, the motor failed. The man who called himself the captain - though he was no more a captain than the boat was a boat - pulled the cord again and again while the others watched in silence. The cord snapped. He sat down heavily, and in his face Amara read the truth he had been keeping from them: he did not know where they were.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'up to "no one will."',
  q1Options:
    "A) The boat was overcrowded.\nB) The passengers had paid to be on the boat.\nC) The boat rode high in the water.\nD) Amara was travelling with her daughter.\nE) The child was crying.\nF) The boat's nails were new.\nG) They were crossing the Atlantic.\nH) Amara was frightened by her daughter's silence.",
  q1Answers45:
    'A, B, D, H - A: forty-three people in a small boat. B: "paid everything they had." D: "her daughter pressed against her chest." H: "This silence frightened Amara more than the sea."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, B, D, H - C is false: "It rode low in the water". E is false: the child "had not cried since they left the coast". F is false: the boat was "held together by nails that were already rusting". G is false: they were on "the Mediterranean".',
  q2Focus: 'from the third paragraph, "The sky was enormous."',
  q2Text: 'to convey the experience of being at sea?',
  q2Answer45:
    'The writer describes the sky as "enormous" and pressing down "with the weight of all that emptiness," which makes the sea feel overwhelming. The sun moves "slowly, indifferently," suggesting nature does not care about the passengers. The motor is compared to "a sick animal" with the simile "coughed and spluttered," showing how unreliable their equipment is. The listing of "no hours, no appointments, no schedules" emphasises how time loses meaning at sea.',
  // FACT-CHECK 2026-04: source attribution corrected per verified-library audit
  q2Answer67:
    'The writer renders the open sea as a space where conventional structures of meaning collapse. The sky\'s enormity is presented not as sublime but as oppressive - it "pressed down" with "the weight of all that emptiness," an oxymoron that transforms void into mass. The sun\'s indifference is conveyed through the adverb "indifferently" - a rare moment of direct emotional attribution to nature that paradoxically emphasises its inhumanity. The tricolon "no hours, no appointments, no schedules" dismantles the temporal framework of civilised life, replacing it with the brutal metric of "fuel remaining." The simile of the motor as "a sick animal" is precisely calibrated: it grants the machine the vulnerability of a living creature, foreshadowing its failure while suggesting the passengers\' survival depends on something as fragile as biological health.',
  q3Text:
    'How has the writer structured the text to create tension and convey the perilousness of the journey?',
  q3Answer45:
    'The opening focuses on how dangerous the boat is, immediately establishing the threat. The second paragraph shifts to Amara and her daughter, giving us characters to care about. The third paragraph widens to the vast empty sea around them. The final paragraph delivers the crisis - the motor failing and the captain revealed as incompetent. Each paragraph escalates the danger.',
  q3Answer67:
    'The writer structures the extract through progressive stripping away of the passengers\' resources - physical, emotional, and epistemological. Paragraph one establishes the material fragility of the vessel; paragraph two narrows to the psychological cost, the child\'s silence functioning as an index of trauma. Paragraph three widens the lens to the overwhelming scale of the sea, a structural expansion that emphasises human insignificance. The temporal leap to "the second night" in the final paragraph compresses time, accelerating toward crisis. The structural climax is not the motor\'s failure but the revelation in the captain\'s face - the extraction of knowledge shifts from material danger to existential uncertainty. The final clause - "he did not know where they were" - strips away the last resource: direction.',
  q4Focus: 'from "The sky was enormous."',
  q4Statement:
    'The writer makes the reader feel the desperation and vulnerability of the people on the boat. The writing is powerful because it treats them with dignity rather than pity.',
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    "I agree. The writer makes us feel the danger through vivid details like the motor coughing and the captain's failure. The description of Amara holding her daughter creates empathy. The narrator treats the passengers with respect - they are not helpless victims but people with courage. The child's silence is the most powerful detail because it shows trauma without sentimentalising it.",
  q4Answer67:
    'I agree strongly. The writer achieves dignity through precision rather than sentimentality. The observation about the child - "A child who cries is a child who believes someone will come. A child who is silent has already learned that no one will" - operates as moral philosophy rather than emotional appeal, elevating the particular to the universal. The "captain" who is "no more a captain than the boat was a boat" is characterised without mockery; the parallel structure acknowledges the collective fiction sustaining the journey. The final image - reading truth in the captain\'s face - positions Amara as perceptive rather than passive. Throughout, the writer refuses the language of victimhood: these are people exercising agency under impossible constraints. The power lies in the gap between what they have risked ("everything they had") and what they have received (a vessel held together by "prayer").',
  q5ImageDesc:
    'Imagine a photograph taken from the deck of a small wooden boat, showing nothing but open sea stretching to the horizon under a vast, cloudless sky.',
  q5NarrativePrompt: 'Write the opening part of a story about a dangerous journey.',
})

const p1_08 = makeP1(8, {
  set: '8',
  source:
    'Thomas Hardy, The Return of the Native (1878; text as revised by Hardy, with his preface of 1895), Book First, Chapter 6, "The Figure against the Sky"',
  extract: `When the whole Egdon concourse had left the site of the bonfire to its accustomed loneliness, a closely wrapped female figure approached the barrow from that quarter of the heath in which the little fire lay. Had the reddleman been watching he might have recognized her as the woman who had first stood there so singularly, and vanished at the approach of strangers. She ascended to her old position at the top, where the red coals of the perishing fire greeted her like living eyes in the corpse of day. There she stood still around her stretching the vast night atmosphere, whose incomplete darkness in comparison with the total darkness of the heath below it might have represented a venial beside a mortal sin.

That she was tall and straight in build, that she was lady-like in her movements, was all that could be learnt of her just now, her form being wrapped in a shawl folded in the old cornerwise fashion, and her head in a large kerchief, a protection not superfluous at this hour and place. Her back was towards the wind, which blew from the northwest; but whether she had avoided that aspect because of the chilly gusts which played about her exceptional position, or because her interest lay in the southeast, did not at first appear.

Her reason for standing so dead still as the pivot of this circle of heath-country was just as obscure. Her extraordinary fixity, her conspicuous loneliness, her heedlessness of night, betokened among other things an utter absence of fear. A tract of country unaltered from that sinister condition which made Cæsar anxious every year to get clear of its glooms before the autumnal equinox, a kind of landscape and weather which leads travellers from the South to describe our island as Homer’s Cimmerian land, was not, on the face of it, friendly to women.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'from the beginning to "a venial beside a mortal sin."',
  q1Options:
    'A) The crowd had left the site of the bonfire.\nB) The figure who approached the barrow was a woman.\nC) The reddleman watched her arrive.\nD) She climbed to the top of the barrow.\nE) The fire had gone completely cold.\nF) It was the middle of the day.\nG) She stood still once she reached the top.\nH) The heath below was lighter than the sky.',
  q1Answers45:
    'A, B, D, G - A: "the whole Egdon concourse had left the site of the bonfire." B: "a closely wrapped female figure approached the barrow." D: "She ascended to her old position at the top." G: "There she stood still."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, B, D, G - C is false: "Had the reddleman been watching" shows that he was not. E is false: "the red coals of the perishing fire" are still glowing. F is false: it is night, and the coals are "like living eyes in the corpse of day." H is false: the sky\'s "incomplete darkness" is set against "the total darkness of the heath below it."',
  q2Focus: 'from "That she was tall and straight in build" to the end.',
  q2Text: 'to present the woman and her relationship with the landscape?',
  q2Answer45:
    'The writer tells us very little about the woman - only that she is "tall and straight in build" and "lady-like in her movements" - which makes her mysterious. She is wrapped in a shawl and "a large kerchief", so we cannot see her clearly. The list "Her extraordinary fixity, her conspicuous loneliness, her heedlessness of night" shows how unusual she is, and the writer says these show "an utter absence of fear". The landscape is described as "sinister" and "not, on the face of it, friendly to women", which makes her lack of fear stand out even more.',
  q2Answer67:
    'Hardy presents the woman almost entirely through what cannot be known. The opening clause admits that "tall and straight in build" and "lady-like in her movements" is "all that could be learnt of her just now", and even her reason for facing away from the wind is left open between two explanations, one practical (the "chilly gusts") and one mysterious (her "interest lay in the southeast"). The phrase "the pivot of this circle of heath-country" places her at the centre of the landscape, as if the heath turns around her. The tricolon "Her extraordinary fixity, her conspicuous loneliness, her heedlessness of night" builds a portrait from abstract nouns rather than physical detail, and the verb "betokened" makes her stillness a sign to be read: it reveals "an utter absence of fear". The final sentence widens to history and myth - "that sinister condition which made Cæsar anxious" and "Homer\'s Cimmerian land" - before the understatement that the landscape "was not, on the face of it, friendly to women". The phrase "on the face of it" hints that this woman, unlike others, may be at home here.',
  q3Text: 'How has the writer structured the text to introduce both the setting and the character?',
  q3Answer45:
    'The extract begins with the crowd leaving the bonfire, so the heath returns to "its accustomed loneliness". Then a single figure appears, and we only learn that it is a woman. The writer describes her climbing to the top of the barrow and standing still, then describes her appearance, and finally the wild landscape around her. The structure keeps her identity hidden, which makes the reader curious about who she is.',
  q3Answer67:
    'Hardy structures the extract as an arrival into emptiness. The first sentence clears the stage: the "whole Egdon concourse" leaves the bonfire "to its accustomed loneliness", and only then does "a closely wrapped female figure" enter. The narrator withholds her identity, noting only that the reddleman "might have recognized her" had he been watching, so the reader is placed in the position of an observer who cannot quite see. Movement then gives way to stillness: she "ascended", then "stood still", and the rest of the extract is spent looking at a figure who does not move. The focus shifts from the sky and heath around her, to her clothes, to her possible motives, and finally out to Caesar and Homer, so that the extract widens from one woman on a barrow to centuries of the landscape\'s reputation. That widening makes her stillness seem more remarkable: the whole history of the place is set against one figure who is not afraid of it.',
  q4Focus: 'from "That she was tall and straight in build" to the end.',
  q4Statement:
    'The writer creates a powerful impression of the woman as someone extraordinary - someone who seems to belong to this wild landscape rather than to be threatened by it.',
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I agree that the woman seems extraordinary. She stands "so dead still" on the barrow at night, and the writer says she shows "an utter absence of fear". She is at the centre of the heath - "the pivot of this circle of heath-country" - which suggests that she belongs there. The landscape is "not, on the face of it, friendly to women", yet she is calm in it, which makes her seem stronger than other people.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I agree, though Hardy makes her belonging to the heath a question rather than a certainty. Her "extraordinary fixity" turns her into part of the scenery: standing "so dead still as the pivot of this circle of heath-country", she is less a visitor to the landscape than its centre. The tricolon of "fixity", "loneliness" and "heedlessness of night" presents qualities that would frighten most people as her natural state, and the narrator reads them as proof of "an utter absence of fear". Yet the details of her dress - "a large kerchief, a protection not superfluous at this hour and place" - remind us that the heath is cold and hostile, and her back is turned to the wind. The final sentence sets her against a landscape that made "Cæsar anxious" and that travellers compared to "Homer\'s Cimmerian land", and the understatement "not, on the face of it, friendly to women" invites the reader to see her as the exception. So she does seem to belong to the landscape, but Hardy suggests that this belonging is itself strange: what makes her extraordinary is that she is at home in a place that should repel her.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    'Imagine a photograph of a vast, dark moorland under a dramatic sky, with a single figure visible on a distant path.',
  q5NarrativePrompt: 'Write the opening part of a story set in a wild, remote landscape.',
})

const p1_09 = makeP1(9, {
  set: '9',
  source: 'Charlotte Perkins Gilman, "The Yellow Wallpaper" (1892)',
  extract: `It is very seldom that mere ordinary people like John and myself secure ancestral halls for the summer.

A colonial mansion, a hereditary estate, I would say a haunted house, and reach the height of romantic felicity—but that would be asking too much of fate!

Still I will proudly declare that there is something queer about it.

Else, why should it be let so cheaply? And why have stood so long untenanted?

John laughs at me, of course, but one expects that in marriage.

John is practical in the extreme. He has no patience with faith, an intense horror of superstition, and he scoffs openly at any talk of things not to be felt and seen and put down in figures.

John is a physician, and perhaps—(I would not say it to a living soul, of course, but this is dead paper and a great relief to my mind)—perhaps that is one reason I do not get well faster.

You see, he does not believe I am sick!

And what can one do?

If a physician of high standing, and one’s own husband, assures friends and relatives that there is really nothing the matter with one but temporary nervous depression—a slight hysterical tendency—what is one to do?

My brother is also a physician, and also of high standing, and he says the same thing.

So I take phosphates or phosphites—whichever it is, and tonics, and journeys, and air, and exercise, and am absolutely forbidden to “work” until I am well again.

Personally, I disagree with their ideas.

Personally, I believe that congenial work, with excitement and change, would do me good.

But what is one to do?

I did write for a while in spite of them; but it does exhaust me a good deal—having to be so sly about it, or else meet with heavy opposition.

I sometimes fancy that in my condition if I had less opposition and more society and stimulus—but John says the very worst thing I can do is to think about my condition, and I confess it always makes me feel bad.

So I will let it alone and talk about the house.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'from the beginning to "perhaps that is one reason I do not get well faster."',
  q1Options:
    'A) The narrator and John are staying in the house for the summer.\nB) The narrator thinks there is something strange about the house.\nC) The house was expensive to rent.\nD) John believes in ghosts.\nE) John laughs at the narrator.\nF) John is a doctor.\nG) The narrator has told other people what she thinks about John.\nH) The narrator is getting well quickly.',
  q1Answers45:
    'A, B, E, F - A: "secure ancestral halls for the summer." B: "there is something queer about it." E: "John laughs at me." F: "John is a physician."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, B, E, F - C is false: the narrator asks why the house should "be let so cheaply". D is false: John has "an intense horror of superstition". G is false: she "would not say it to a living soul". H is false: she says "I do not get well faster".',
  q2Focus: 'from "If a physician of high standing" to the end.',
  q2Text: "to create a sense of unease beneath the narrator's calm tone?",
  q2Answer45:
    'The narrator sounds calm, but the writer shows that something is wrong. Her husband and her brother are both doctors "of high standing", which makes it hard for her to argue with them. The question "what is one to do?" makes her sound helpless. The list of her treatment, "tonics, and journeys, and air, and exercise", shows that other people are deciding everything for her, and she does not even know whether she takes "phosphates or phosphites". She says "Personally, I disagree with their ideas", but she has to write "in spite of them" and be "so sly about it", which is worrying because she has to hide what she does. At the end she stops herself: "So I will let it alone and talk about the house."',
  q2Answer67:
    'Gilman creates unease through the gap between what the narrator says and what her sentences reveal. The conditional opening, "If a physician of high standing, and one\'s own husband", stacks up authority before she has made her point, so that her own view arrives already outweighed. The diagnosis is reported in the doctors\' soothing terms, "temporary nervous depression" and "a slight hysterical tendency", and the modifiers "temporary" and "slight" play her illness down. Her helplessness surfaces in the refrain "what is one to do?", repeated as "But what is one to do?": the impersonal "one" distances her from her own situation, as if she may not say "I". The polysyndeton of "tonics, and journeys, and air, and exercise" makes the treatment sound endless, and "whichever it is" shows that nobody has told her what she is taking. The strongest phrase in the passage, "absolutely forbidden", is applied to "work", which she places in inverted commas as if quoting the men who forbid it. Against this, her two short sentences beginning "Personally," are the only places where she speaks for herself, and even her writing has to be done "in spite of them". The unease peaks when her longest thought, "if I had less opposition and more society and stimulus", is cut off by a dash and by her husband: "but John says the very worst thing I can do is to think about my condition". His voice interrupts hers even on the page, and the calm final sentence, "So I will let it alone and talk about the house", shows her obeying him in private.',
  q3Text: "How has the writer structured the text to gradually reveal the narrator's situation?",
  q3Answer45:
    'The extract begins light-heartedly with the house, which the narrator jokes might be "a haunted house". Then the focus moves to her husband, John, who "laughs at me". In the middle of the extract we learn that she is ill and that John "does not believe I am sick". Many of the paragraphs are only one sentence long, which makes the extract feel like a diary. The question "what is one to do?" is asked three times in different forms, showing that she feels trapped. At the end she says she will "talk about the house", so the extract goes back to where it started, as if she is avoiding the real problem.',
  q3Answer67:
    'Gilman structures the extract as a movement inward and then a retreat. It opens on the house, with the playful exaggeration of "ancestral halls" and the half-wish for "a haunted house", so the reader expects a ghost story. The focus then narrows to John, who "laughs at me", and a parenthesis reveals what kind of text this is: "this is dead paper and a great relief to my mind". These are private notes that nobody is meant to read. Only now does the real situation emerge: the narrator is ill, and her husband "does not believe I am sick". The very short paragraphs, many of them a single sentence, create the rhythm of a diary written in snatches, and a refrain of questions ("And what can one do?", "what is one to do?", "But what is one to do?") marks each point at which she gives up an argument. The climax is her fullest statement of her own needs, which breaks off in mid-sentence when John\'s opinion takes over. The last sentence, "So I will let it alone and talk about the house", returns to the opening subject, and this circular structure is itself revealing: she retreats into safe description because she is not allowed to think about her condition.',
  q4Focus: 'from "If a physician of high standing" to the end.',
  q4Statement:
    'The most disturbing thing about this extract is not what the narrator reveals, but how calmly she reveals it. The acceptance is more frightening than any protest would be.',
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I agree. The narrator describes her illness and her treatment calmly, as if they were ordinary, which is disturbing. She lists what she has to take, "tonics, and journeys, and air, and exercise", without complaining. She does say "Personally, I disagree with their ideas", so she protests a little, but only in her private writing. She keeps asking "what is one to do?", which shows that she has accepted that she cannot change anything. At the end she decides to "let it alone and talk about the house", which is the most worrying part because she gives up.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I largely agree, although the extract is more divided than the statement allows. Much of the disturbance does come from the narrator\'s calm. She reports that her husband and her brother, both physicians "of high standing", have dismissed her illness as "temporary nervous depression", and she passes on their verdict in their own reassuring words rather than hers. Her treatment is listed in the same even tone, and the fact that she does not know whether she takes "phosphates or phosphites" suggests a patient who has handed her body over to others. Yet she does protest. The two sentences beginning "Personally," are firm, and she believes that "congenial work, with excitement and change, would do me good". What disturbs is how quickly each protest is withdrawn: "But what is one to do?" follows at once. Her one act of resistance, writing "in spite of them", has to be done in secret, and it "does exhaust me a good deal". So I would adjust the statement: what is most frightening is not simple acceptance but a protest that is made in private and then abandoned. The final sentence, "So I will let it alone and talk about the house", shows her obeying John\'s rule that "the very worst thing I can do is to think about my condition" even as she writes.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    'Imagine a photograph of a large old country house standing alone at the end of a long drive, with bars across its upstairs windows.',
  q5NarrativePrompt:
    'Write the opening part of a story told by a narrator who gradually reveals something disturbing about their world.',
})

const p1_10 = makeP1(10, {
  set: '10',
  source: 'Anne Brontë, Agnes Grey (1847), Chapter 2, "First Lessons in the Art of Instruction"',
  extract: `As we drove along, my spirits revived again, and I turned, with pleasure, to the contemplation of the new life upon which I was entering. But though it was not far past the middle of September, the heavy clouds and strong north-easterly wind combined to render the day extremely cold and dreary; and the journey seemed a very long one, for, as Smith observed, the roads were “very heavy”; and certainly, his horse was very heavy too: it crawled up the hills, and crept down them, and only condescended to shake its sides in a trot where the road was at a dead level or a very gentle slope, which was rarely the case in those rugged regions; so that it was nearly one o’clock before we reached the place of our destination. Yet, after all, when we entered the lofty iron gateway, when we drove softly up the smooth, well-rolled carriage-road, with the green lawn on each side, studded with young trees, and approached the new but stately mansion of Wellwood, rising above its mushroom poplar-groves, my heart failed me, and I wished it were a mile or two farther off. For the first time in my life I must stand alone: there was no retreating now. I must enter that house, and introduce myself among its strange inhabitants. But how was it to be done? True, I was near nineteen; but, thanks to my retired life and the protecting care of my mother and sister, I well knew that many a girl of fifteen, or under, was gifted with a more womanly address, and greater ease and self-possession, than I was. Yet, if Mrs. Bloomfield were a kind, motherly woman, I might do very well, after all; and the children, of course, I should soon be at ease with them—and Mr. Bloomfield, I hoped, I should have but little to do with.

“Be calm, be calm, whatever happens,” I said within myself; and truly I kept this resolution so well, and was so fully occupied in steadying my nerves and stifling the rebellious flutter of my heart, that when I was admitted into the hall and ushered into the presence of Mrs. Bloomfield, I almost forgot to answer her polite salutation; and it afterwards struck me, that the little I did say was spoken in the tone of one half-dead or half-asleep. The lady, too, was somewhat chilly in her manner, as I discovered when I had time to reflect. She was a tall, spare, stately woman, with thick black hair, cold grey eyes, and extremely sallow complexion.`,
  q1TrueStatements:
    'from the beginning to "nearly one o’clock before we reached the place of our destination."',
  q1Options:
    "A) The narrator's spirits rose as the journey began.\nB) The journey took place in the middle of summer.\nC) The day was cold and dreary.\nD) The wind was blowing from the south.\nE) The horse travelled quickly.\nF) The driver was called Smith.\nG) The journey felt short.\nH) They arrived at nearly one o’clock.",
  q1Answers45:
    'A, C, F, H - A: "my spirits revived again." C: "extremely cold and dreary." F: "as Smith observed." H: "it was nearly one o’clock before we reached the place of our destination."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, C, F, H - B is false: it was "not far past the middle of September". D is false: the wind was "north-easterly". E is false: the horse "crawled up the hills, and crept down them". G is false: "the journey seemed a very long one".',
  q2Focus:
    'from "Yet, after all, when we entered the lofty iron gateway" to "Mr. Bloomfield, I hoped, I should have but little to do with."',
  q2Text: "to convey the narrator's feelings as she arrives?",
  q2Answer45:
    'The writer shows that the narrator is very nervous. The long sentence describing the drive up to the house, with its "lofty iron gateway" and "stately mansion", makes the house seem grand and frightening, and it ends with "my heart failed me". She wishes the house were "a mile or two farther off", which shows that she wants to put off arriving. The short question "But how was it to be done?" shows her panic. She says that many "a girl of fifteen, or under" had more "ease and self-possession" than she did, which shows that she feels young and unready. At the end she tries to cheer herself up by hoping that Mrs. Bloomfield will be "a kind, motherly woman".',
  q2Answer67:
    'Brontë conveys the narrator\'s feelings through the shape of her sentences as much as her words. The approach to the house is one long sentence whose clauses, "when we entered the lofty iron gateway, when we drove softly up the smooth, well-rolled carriage-road", pile up grandeur and delay the main verb, so that the house seems to grow while the reader waits, until the sentence turns on "my heart failed me". The adjectives "lofty", "smooth" and "stately" suggest a wealth and order that intimidate rather than welcome her, and the "mushroom poplar-groves" hint that the estate is newly made. After this the sentences shrink. "For the first time in my life I must stand alone: there was no retreating now" uses the modal verb "must" and the military language of "retreating" to present the arrival as a battle she cannot avoid, and the short question "But how was it to be done?" exposes her panic. Her judgement of herself is painfully honest: at "near nineteen" she believes that many younger girls have "greater ease and self-possession". The paragraph ends in hope, but a fragile hope built on a conditional: "if Mrs. Bloomfield were a kind, motherly woman, I might do very well, after all".',
  q3Text:
    'How has the writer structured the text to establish mood and prepare the reader for what is to come?',
  q3Answer45:
    'The extract begins hopefully: the narrator\'s "spirits revived again". The mood then becomes gloomier, because the weather is "cold and dreary" and the journey is slow. When she arrives at the grand house her feelings change, and "my heart failed me". The writer then shows her thoughts as she worries about meeting the family. The second paragraph moves on to the meeting with Mrs. Bloomfield, who is "somewhat chilly in her manner". The extract ends by describing her "cold grey eyes", which prepares the reader for an unfriendly employer.',
  q3Answer67:
    'Brontë structures the extract as a journey from hope to apprehension that ends by bringing the narrator face to face with the cause of her fear. The opening sentence looks forward with "pleasure" to "the new life", but the weather darkens the mood at once, and the slow horse that "crawled up the hills" stretches the journey out, delaying the arrival much as the narrator would like to. The arrival is the turning point: the long sentence through the gateway and up the drive ends at "my heart failed me", and from there the focus moves inward, to her fears and her attempts to reassure herself. The second paragraph changes pace again. Her resolution, "Be calm, be calm, whatever happens", is followed by the meeting she has dreaded, which passes almost without her noticing, and only "when I had time to reflect" does she judge it. The last sentence is a portrait of Mrs. Bloomfield, and its details, "cold grey eyes" and an "extremely sallow complexion", confirm the chill that the weather first suggested. By ending at the moment of meeting, the extract leaves the reader expecting that her fears were justified.',
  q4Focus: 'from "For the first time in my life I must stand alone" to the end.',
  q4Statement:
    'The writer creates a powerful sense of a young woman facing an uncertain future with quiet courage, and the reader cannot help but feel anxious for her.',
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I agree. The narrator knows that she "must stand alone" for the first time, which shows that she is facing something new and uncertain. She is brave because she tells herself "Be calm, be calm, whatever happens" instead of running away. She is honest about feeling unready, saying that younger girls have more "self-possession" than she does. I feel anxious for her because Mrs. Bloomfield is "somewhat chilly in her manner" and has "cold grey eyes", so it does not look as if she will be welcomed.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I largely agree, although the courage is quieter, and more comic, than the statement suggests. The narrator\'s situation is genuinely uncertain: "For the first time in my life I must stand alone" presents her as newly independent, and "there was no retreating now" turns a new job into a test of nerve. Her courage lies in going on despite her doubts. She admits that, although "near nineteen", she lacks the "womanly address" of younger girls, and she steadies herself with the repeated command "Be calm, be calm, whatever happens". Yet Brontë also invites a gentle smile at her: she is so busy "stifling the rebellious flutter of my heart" that she almost forgets to answer her employer, and her voice comes out like that of "one half-dead or half-asleep". The reader\'s anxiety is sharpened by her hopes. She pins everything on Mrs. Bloomfield being "a kind, motherly woman", and the extract ends by quietly dashing that hope with a portrait of "cold grey eyes". So the reader does feel anxious for her, but the feeling comes as much from the older narrator\'s wry honesty about her younger self as from any open threat.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    'Imagine a photograph of a long, straight drive leading between young trees to a large, grand house under a grey sky.',
  q5NarrativePrompt:
    'Write the opening part of a story about a character travelling to an unfamiliar place.',
})

const p1_11 = makeP1(11, {
  set: '11',
  source:
    "Original composition in the style of Andrea Levy's Small Island (in-copyright; do not cite as Levy)",
  extract: `The cold hit me like a wall. Not the cold I knew from home - the cool breeze that blew in off the sea in the evenings, carrying the scent of salt and frangipani - but a cold that had weight and substance, that pressed against your face and crept inside your clothes and settled in your bones like it meant to stay. I stepped off the gangway and onto English soil - though it was not soil but concrete, grey and wet and entirely without charm - and I thought: so this is it. This is the Mother Country.

I had expected grandeur. I had expected the England of my schoolbooks - the rolling green hills, the ancient churches, the gentlemen in top hats tipping their brims to ladies in white. I had expected, at the very least, a welcome. Instead I found Tilbury Docks on a Tuesday morning in November, and a sky the colour of old dishwater, and a man in a uniform who looked at my passport, looked at me, and looked at my passport again as though the two could not possibly belong together.

"Purpose of visit?" he said.

I wanted to say: I have come to help rebuild your country. I have come because you asked us to come. I have come because I was taught that this place was home, and I believed it. Instead I said: "Work."

He stamped the passport without looking at me again. And I walked through the gate into England, carrying everything I owned in a single brown suitcase, and the word "work" still hanging in the air behind me - small and flat and inadequate, like a key that no longer fits its lock.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'up to "This is the Mother Country."',
  q1Options:
    "A) The narrator came from a warm country.\nB) The narrator arrived by ship.\nC) The ground was muddy.\nD) The narrator was disappointed.\nE) England matched the narrator's expectations.\nF) The weather was cold.\nG) The narrator arrived in autumn.\nH) The narrator felt immediately at home.",
  q1Answers45:
    'A, B, D, F - A: references to "sea," "salt and frangipani" indicate a tropical homeland. B: "stepped off the gangway." D: "entirely without charm" and the ironic tone suggest disappointment. F: "The cold hit me like a wall."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, B, D, F - C is false (the ground is "concrete"). E is the opposite. G is true ("November") but falls slightly after the cut-off depending on reading. H is false - the tone is disillusioned.',
  q2Focus: 'from "I had expected grandeur" to the end of the extract.',
  q2Text: "to convey the narrator's experience of arriving in England?",
  q2Answer45:
    'The writer uses the repetition of "I had expected" to build up what the narrator hoped for, making the disappointment worse. The passport officer looking at the passport, then at the narrator, then back shows racial suspicion without saying it directly. The simile at the end - "like a key that no longer fits its lock" - suggests the narrator feels she does not belong in a place she was taught was home.',
  q2Answer67:
    'Levy constructs the arrival as a progressive deflation of colonial mythology. The anaphoric "I had expected" creates a rhetorical crescendo of imagined England - "rolling green hills," "ancient churches," "gentlemen in top hats" - each image drawn from imperial iconography, its accumulated idealism intensifying the bathos of "Tilbury Docks on a Tuesday morning in November." The passport scene enacts racial scrutiny through a devastating tricolon of looking: "looked at my passport, looked at me, and looked at my passport again." The rhythm of repetition performs the officer\'s disbelief that this face belongs to this document. The internal monologue - "I have come because you asked us to come" - uses the second person to reverse the colonial gaze, positioning England as the supplicant, but the actually spoken "Work" reduces this complex history to a single monosyllable. The final simile - "like a key that no longer fits its lock" - transforms the word "work" into a failed mechanism of belonging: the narrator has the credential but the door will not open.',
  q3Text:
    "How has the writer structured the text to convey the narrator's journey from expectation to reality?",
  q3Answer45:
    'The extract opens with the shock of cold, immediately establishing that England is not what was expected. The second paragraph lists what was expected, creating contrast. The dialogue with the passport officer shows the hostile reality. The final paragraph reveals the narrator walking into England alone with just a suitcase - a powerful image of isolation. The structure moves from hope to disappointment to quiet determination.',
  q3Answer67:
    "Levy structures the extract as a systematic dismantling of the colonial promise. The opening paragraph performs the initial sensory shock - cold as \"wall,\" concrete instead of soil - establishing the body as the first register of disillusionment. The second paragraph shifts to ideology, the gap between schoolbook England and actual England functioning as the text's central structural opposition. The dialogue scene compresses the political into the personal: the passport officer's scrutiny and the narrator's truncated response enact the power imbalance of the postcolonial encounter. The final paragraph's image - one person, one suitcase, one gate - uses structural simplification to convey emotional desolation. The simile that closes the extract looks backward (the key that once fit) and forward (it no longer does), providing structural ambiguity about whether this is a story of arrival or exclusion.",
  q4Focus: 'from "\'Purpose of visit?\' he said" to the end.',
  q4Statement:
    "The writer powerfully conveys the narrator's sense of injustice - that she has come to a country that claimed to be her home but treats her as a stranger.",
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I agree. The narrator was taught "this place was home" and "you asked us to come," which shows she believed England wanted her. But the passport officer\'s suspicious looks and cold manner show the reality. The word "Work" is all she says, even though she wants to say much more. The gap between what she thinks and what she says shows how powerless she feels. The suitcase detail - "everything I owned" - shows she has committed fully to a country that will not commit to her.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I agree strongly. Levy constructs the injustice through the structural gap between interior and exterior speech - what the narrator thinks versus what she says. The unspoken sentences - "I have come to help rebuild your country. I have come because you asked us to come" - use anaphoric "I have come" to build a moral argument the narrator cannot articulate in the moment. The power imbalance is encoded in the scene\'s dynamics: the officer asks, stamps, and turns away; the narrator answers, walks through, and carries her inadequacy with her. The simile of the key is devastating because it acknowledges prior belonging - the key once fit - while registering its present failure. The injustice is not simply that the narrator is unwelcome but that the terms of welcome were established by the very people now withholding it. However, the narrator\'s dignity is preserved: she does not beg or explain but walks through the gate, and the writing\'s anger is controlled, which makes it more powerful.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    "Imagine a photograph of a busy dockside on a grey morning, with passengers descending a ship's gangway carrying luggage.",
  q5NarrativePrompt:
    'Write the opening part of a story about arriving somewhere new and finding it different from what you expected.',
})

const p1_12 = makeP1(12, {
  set: '12',
  source:
    'Charles Dickens, David Copperfield (1849-50), Chapter 11, "I Begin Life on My Own Account, and Don\'t Like It"',
  extract: `I know enough of the world now, to have almost lost the capacity of being much surprised by anything; but it is matter of some surprise to me, even now, that I can have been so easily thrown away at such an age. A child of excellent abilities, and with strong powers of observation, quick, eager, delicate, and soon hurt bodily or mentally, it seems wonderful to me that nobody should have made any sign in my behalf. But none was made; and I became, at ten years old, a little labouring hind in the service of Murdstone and Grinby.

Murdstone and Grinby’s warehouse was at the waterside. It was down in Blackfriars. Modern improvements have altered the place; but it was the last house at the bottom of a narrow street, curving down hill to the river, with some stairs at the end, where people took boat. It was a crazy old house with a wharf of its own, abutting on the water when the tide was in, and on the mud when the tide was out, and literally overrun with rats. Its panelled rooms, discoloured with the dirt and smoke of a hundred years, I dare say; its decaying floors and staircase; the squeaking and scuffling of the old grey rats down in the cellars; and the dirt and rottenness of the place; are things, not of many years ago, in my mind, but of the present instant. They are all before me, just as they were in the evil hour when I went among them for the first time, with my trembling hand in Mr. Quinion’s.

Murdstone and Grinby’s trade was among a good many kinds of people, but an important branch of it was the supply of wines and spirits to certain packet ships. I forget now where they chiefly went, but I think there were some among them that made voyages both to the East and West Indies. I know that a great many empty bottles were one of the consequences of this traffic, and that certain men and boys were employed to examine them against the light, and reject those that were flawed, and to rinse and wash them. When the empty bottles ran short, there were labels to be pasted on full ones, or corks to be fitted to them, or seals to be put upon the corks, or finished bottles to be packed in casks. All this work was my work, and of the boys employed upon it I was one.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'from the beginning to "literally overrun with rats."',
  q1Options:
    "A) The narrator is an adult looking back on his childhood.\nB) The narrator was ten years old when he began work.\nC) Several people spoke up on the narrator's behalf.\nD) The narrator describes himself as a slow and dull child.\nE) The warehouse was beside the river.\nF) The warehouse was a modern building.\nG) The warehouse was overrun with rats.\nH) The warehouse stood at the top of a hill.",
  q1Answers45:
    'A, B, E, G - A: "I know enough of the world now." B: "at ten years old." E: "at the waterside." G: "literally overrun with rats."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, B, E, G - C is false: "nobody should have made any sign in my behalf". D is false: he calls himself "A child of excellent abilities". F is false: it was "a crazy old house". H is false: it was "the last house at the bottom of a narrow street, curving down hill to the river".',
  q2Focus: 'from "It was a crazy old house" to "with my trembling hand in Mr. Quinion’s."',
  q2Text: 'to describe the warehouse?',
  q2Answer45:
    'The writer describes the warehouse as "a crazy old house", which makes it sound as if it is falling apart. It is "literally overrun with rats", and the "squeaking and scuffling" of the rats helps the reader to hear how unpleasant it was. The phrase "the dirt and rottenness of the place" shows how filthy and decayed it was. The narrator calls his first day "the evil hour", which makes the place sound almost wicked, and his "trembling hand" shows how frightened he was.',
  q2Answer67:
    'Dickens presents the warehouse as a place of decay that has lodged permanently in the narrator\'s mind. The adjective "crazy" suggests a building so old and crooked that it seems almost unhinged, and the detail that it stands on the water "when the tide was in" and "on the mud when the tide was out" makes it feel unstable, never on solid ground. The adverb in "literally overrun with rats" insists that this is no exaggeration. The long sentence beginning "Its panelled rooms" is built from noun phrases separated by semicolons: rooms "discoloured with the dirt and smoke of a hundred years", "decaying floors and staircase", and rats "squeaking and scuffling" in the cellars, where the sibilance and onomatopoeia let the reader hear the place as well as see it. The list ends in the abstract nouns "dirt and rottenness", as if decay were the building\'s very nature, and the main verb arrives only at the end: these things are "not of many years ago, in my mind, but of the present instant". The phrase "the evil hour" gives the day an almost moral darkness, and the final image of the boy\'s "trembling hand" held in Mr. Quinion\'s shrinks the huge, rotten building down to one frightened child.',
  q3Text: 'How has the writer structured the text to create a contrast between past and present?',
  q3Answer45:
    'The extract begins in the present, with the adult narrator saying "I know enough of the world now". He then goes back to the time when he was "ten years old" and began work. The writer describes the warehouse, and says that "Modern improvements have altered the place", which shows the difference between then and now. He says the memories are "of the present instant", which shows that the past still feels real to him. The extract ends with the work he did, so we finish in the past, with the child.',
  q3Answer67:
    'Dickens structures the extract around the double perspective of the adult narrator and the child he was. It opens in the present tense with a worldly man who has "almost lost the capacity of being much surprised by anything", yet is still surprised that he could be "so easily thrown away at such an age"; the contrast between the knowing adult and the abandoned boy is set up before any event is told. The first paragraph then moves from reflection to fact: "I became, at ten years old, a little labouring hind". The second paragraph places the warehouse in space and time, and the admission that "Modern improvements have altered the place" marks the distance between then and now. Yet the same paragraph closes that distance: the rooms and rats are "not of many years ago, in my mind, but of the present instant", and "They are all before me". The final paragraph adopts a detached, factual tone, and the adult\'s vagueness ("I forget now where they chiefly went") contrasts with the vividness of the rats, suggesting that what he remembers is the suffering, not the business. The extract closes on the child absorbed into the workforce: "of the boys employed upon it I was one".',
  q4Focus: 'from "It was a crazy old house" to the end.',
  q4Statement:
    'In this part of the extract, the writer makes the warehouse seem a place of horror, and the reader cannot help feeling that the boy has been abandoned there.',
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I agree. The warehouse is a horrible place: it is "a crazy old house", "overrun with rats" and full of "dirt and rottenness". The narrator calls his first day "the evil hour", which makes it sound frightening. I feel that the boy has been abandoned, because his "trembling hand" is held by Mr. Quinion rather than by a parent. The work is described as dull and repetitive, and the last sentence, "All this work was my work", shows that he has simply become one of the workers.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I largely agree, though Dickens creates the horror more quietly than the statement suggests. The warehouse is certainly a place of horror: it is "a crazy old house", "literally overrun with rats", and the list of its "decaying floors and staircase", its rats "squeaking and scuffling" and "the dirt and rottenness of the place" builds a sense of physical disgust. The phrase "the evil hour" gives the memory a moral darkness, and the claim that these things are "of the present instant" shows that the horror has never left him. The sense of abandonment rests on a single image: the child\'s "trembling hand" is held not by a parent but by Mr. Quinion. The final paragraph, however, is not horrified at all. It describes the trade in a calm, almost businesslike voice ("I forget now where they chiefly went") and lists the tasks of rinsing, labelling, corking and packing without comment. This flatness is arguably more disturbing than the rats: the child is absorbed into the routine, and the last sentence, "All this work was my work, and of the boys employed upon it I was one", records his abandonment as a plain fact. So the reader does feel that he has been abandoned, but the strongest feeling comes from how ordinary the adult narrator makes it sound.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    'Imagine a photograph of an old, crumbling warehouse beside a river at low tide, its windows dark and its wharf deep in mud.',
  q5NarrativePrompt:
    "Write the opening part of a story about a young person's first day in a place they did not choose to be.",
})

const p1_13 = makeP1(13, {
  set: '13',
  source:
    "Original composition in the style of Patrick Gale's Notes from an Exhibition (in-copyright; do not cite as Gale)",
  extract: `The sea was the colour of slate, and the rain fell straight down into it without any wind to give it direction. Rachel stood at the window of the studio and watched the water meet itself - sea and sky joining in a grey communion that erased the horizon entirely. On days like this, the peninsula felt less like land and more like a raft, floating unanchored between elements.

She had not painted in three weeks. The canvases leaned against the wall behind her, their faces turned away as though in reproach, and the tubes of paint on the trestle table had begun to develop that cracked, dried look that materials get when they have been neglected. Her brushes stood in a jar like dead flowers. Everything in the studio spoke of abandonment, and she was the one who had abandoned it.

The problem was not inspiration. The problem was that she had too much of it - a surplus that jammed the mechanism, the way too many letters crammed into a postbox will block the slot. She could see what she wanted to paint: the light on the water at Lamorna, the way the gorse caught fire in the late afternoon, the particular quality of shadow beneath the granite hedgerows. She could see it all with a clarity that was almost painful. But between the seeing and the doing, something had broken - some essential connection between eye and hand, between intention and execution - and she did not know how to repair it.

She picked up a brush. Dipped it in cadmium yellow. Held it above the canvas. And put it down again.

Four gestures. A complete failure narrated in twelve words.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'up to "floating unanchored between elements."',
  q1Options:
    'A) The sea was bright blue.\nB) It was raining.\nC) Rachel was in a studio.\nD) The horizon was clearly visible.\nE) A strong wind was blowing.\nF) Rachel was painting.\nG) The setting was coastal.\nH) The landscape felt isolated.',
  q1Answers45:
    'B, C, G, H - B: "the rain fell straight down." C: "Rachel stood at the window of the studio." G: "the peninsula." H: "floating unanchored between elements."',
  q1Answers67:
    'B, C, G, H - A is false: the sea was "the colour of slate". D is false: sea and sky "erased the horizon entirely". E is false: the rain fell "without any wind to give it direction". F is false: Rachel "watched the water"; she was not painting.',
  q2Focus: 'from "She had not painted in three weeks" to "she was the one who had abandoned it."',
  q2Text: "to convey Rachel's creative paralysis?",
  q2Answer45:
    'The canvases are personified as having "their faces turned away as though in reproach," making Rachel feel guilty. The simile "like dead flowers" for the brushes suggests her art has died. The phrase "everything in the studio spoke of abandonment" is powerful because it makes Rachel both the victim and the cause of the neglect.',
  q2Answer67:
    'Gale presents creative paralysis as a form of self-inflicted bereavement. The canvases\' faces "turned away as though in reproach" personify the unfinished work as a relationship Rachel has damaged, the verb "reproach" implying moral failing. The simile "like dead flowers" for the brushes operates on multiple registers: flowers are both natural and decorative, suggesting art that once bloomed and has now withered. The dried, cracked paint tubes function metonymically - the materials\' decay mirrors the artist\'s interior state. The final sentence of the paragraph performs a structural turn: "she was the one who had abandoned it" shifts agency from the studio to Rachel, transforming what appeared to be external desolation into self-accusation. The reflexive nature of this abandonment - the artist abandoning art - creates a paradox the text leaves unresolved.',
  q3Text: 'How has the writer structured the text to build towards the final moment of failure?',
  q3Answer45:
    "The extract begins with the landscape, then moves to the studio, then to Rachel's inner thoughts, then to a single physical action. Each paragraph gets closer to the act of painting. The third paragraph explains the problem - she can see what to paint but cannot do it. The four short sentences in the penultimate paragraph mirror the four gestures. The final sentence provides commentary, making the failure explicit.",
  q3Answer67:
    'Gale structures the extract as an approach toward artistic creation that mirrors and then enacts its failure. The opening paragraph establishes the external world as undifferentiated - "grey communion," erased horizon - reflecting Rachel\'s internal blankness. Paragraph two documents the evidence of inactivity. Paragraph three performs a crucial structural twist: it reveals that the problem is surplus, not deficit, the simile of the jammed postbox transforming inspiration from resource to obstacle. The penultimate paragraph - four staccato sentences, four discrete actions - is structurally revolutionary: it reduces the narrative to a sequence of verbs (picked, dipped, held, put down) that mime the attempted creative process. The final sentence\'s self-aware commentary - "A complete failure narrated in twelve words" - breaks the fourth wall, the metafictional voice acknowledging that the writing is doing what the painting cannot.',
  q4Focus: 'from "The problem was not inspiration."',
  q4Statement:
    'The writer powerfully captures the frustration of creative block - the agony of being able to see clearly what you want to create but being unable to make it real.',
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I agree. The writer shows that Rachel can see "with a clarity that was almost painful" what she wants to paint, but "something had broken" between seeing and doing. The four short sentences where she picks up and puts down the brush are very effective because they show the attempt and failure simply. The final sentence - "A complete failure narrated in twelve words" - is honest and sad.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I agree strongly, and I would argue that Gale\'s achievement is making the absence of creation as vivid as creation itself. The simile of the jammed postbox is precisely calibrated: it reframes the problem as mechanical rather than inspirational, suggesting that creative block is not emptiness but overflow, a paradox that any artist would recognise. The phrase "between the seeing and the doing, something had broken" locates the failure in the gap between perception and execution - the synapse between eye and hand. The four-sentence sequence is the text\'s structural triumph: its extreme compression mirrors the reduction of artistic ambition to gesture, and the verb "put it down" carries the weight of everything the preceding paragraphs have described. The final metafictional sentence is both devastating and oddly liberating: by narrating failure so precisely, the writer achieves through words what Rachel cannot achieve through paint, suggesting that creative expression will find a way even when its intended medium fails.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    "Imagine a photograph of an artist's studio with a blank canvas on an easel, paint-stained tables, and a rain-streaked window overlooking a grey sea.",
  q5NarrativePrompt:
    'Write the opening part of a story about a character struggling to do something they used to find easy.',
})

const p1_14 = makeP1(14, {
  set: '14',
  source:
    'Robert Louis Stevenson, Kidnapped (1886), Chapter 20, "The Flight in the Heather: The Rocks"',
  extract: `The first peep of morning, then, showed us this horrible place, and I could see Alan knit his brow.

“This is no fit place for you and me,” he said. “This is a place they’re bound to watch.”

And with that he ran harder than ever down to the water-side, in a part where the river was split in two among three rocks. It went through with a horrid thundering that made my belly quake; and there hung over the lynn a little mist of spray. Alan looked neither to the right nor to the left, but jumped clean upon the middle rock and fell there on his hands and knees to check himself, for that rock was small and he might have pitched over on the far side. I had scarce time to measure the distance or to understand the peril before I had followed him, and he had caught and stopped me.

So there we stood, side by side upon a small rock slippery with spray, a far broader leap in front of us, and the river dinning upon all sides. When I saw where I was, there came on me a deadly sickness of fear, and I put my hand over my eyes. Alan took me and shook me; I saw he was speaking, but the roaring of the falls and the trouble of my mind prevented me from hearing; only I saw his face was red with anger, and that he stamped upon the rock. The same look showed me the water raging by, and the mist hanging in the air: and with that I covered my eyes again and shuddered.

The next minute Alan had set the brandy bottle to my lips, and forced me to drink about a gill, which sent the blood into my head again. Then, putting his hands to his mouth, and his mouth to my ear, he shouted, “Hang or drown!” and turning his back upon me, leaped over the farther branch of the stream, and landed safe.

I was now alone upon the rock, which gave me the more room; the brandy was singing in my ears; I had this good example fresh before me, and just wit enough to see that if I did not leap at once, I should never leap at all. I bent low on my knees and flung myself forth, with that kind of anger of despair that has sometimes stood me in stead of courage. Sure enough, it was but my hands that reached the full length; these slipped, caught again, slipped again; and I was sliddering back into the lynn, when Alan seized me, first by the hair, then by the collar, and with a great strain dragged me into safety.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'from the beginning to "he had caught and stopped me."',
  q1Options:
    'A) The events take place at dawn.\nB) Alan was pleased with the place they had reached.\nC) Alan thought the place would be watched.\nD) The river was split among three rocks.\nE) The river was calm and quiet.\nF) Alan jumped onto the middle rock.\nG) The narrator jumped first.\nH) The narrator had plenty of time to judge the distance.',
  q1Answers45:
    'A, C, D, F - A: "The first peep of morning." C: "This is a place they\'re bound to watch." D: "split in two among three rocks." F: "jumped clean upon the middle rock."',
  q1Answers67:
    'A, C, D, F - B is false: Alan "knit his brow" at "this horrible place." E is false: the water went through "with a horrid thundering." G is false: the narrator "had followed him." H is false: "I had scarce time to measure the distance or to understand the peril."',
  q2Focus: 'from "So there we stood" to the end.',
  q2Text: "to create a sense of danger and the narrator's physical peril?",
  q2Answer45:
    'The rock is "small" and "slippery with spray", which makes the danger clear straight away. The narrator feels "a deadly sickness of fear" and covers his eyes, showing he is terrified. The noise is so loud that he cannot hear Alan because of "the roaring of the falls". The most frightening moment is when his hands "slipped, caught again, slipped again", which makes the reader feel he could fall at any second, until Alan seizes him "first by the hair, then by the collar".',
  q2Answer67:
    'Stevenson builds danger through the senses and then takes them away one by one. The pair stand on "a small rock slippery with spray" with "a far broader leap in front of us", so the peril is set out in the first sentence, while "the river dinning upon all sides" surrounds them with noise. Fear becomes physical in "a deadly sickness of fear", and the narrator\'s gesture of covering his eyes, repeated when he "covered my eyes again and shuddered", shows him trying to shut out what he cannot escape. Sound is taken away too: he sees Alan speaking, but "the roaring of the falls and the trouble of my mind" prevent him from hearing, so outer noise and inner panic become one. Alan\'s blunt "Hang or drown!" reduces the choice to death either way. The leap itself is told in a rush of short clauses - "these slipped, caught again, slipped again" - whose rhythm mimics hands losing their grip, and the Scots word "sliddering" makes the fall towards the lynn sound uncontrolled. Rescue arrives "first by the hair, then by the collar": even safety is violent.',
  q3Text: 'How has the writer structured the text to create and escalate tension?',
  q3Answer45:
    'The extract begins at dawn with Alan worried about "this horrible place", which warns us that something bad could happen. Alan\'s short speech explains the danger of being seen. Then the pair run to the river, and each paragraph brings a new danger: the first jump, standing on the rock, Alan leaping away, and finally the narrator\'s own leap. Leaving the narrator "alone upon the rock" makes the tension highest just before the end.',
  q3Answer67:
    'Stevenson structures the extract as a series of thresholds, each more dangerous than the last. The opening sentence creates a mood of threat through Alan\'s knitted brow before any danger is named, and his two-sentence speech supplies the reason: "This is a place they\'re bound to watch." The first leap happens almost before the narrator can think - "I had scarce time to measure the distance or to understand the peril" - so the reader, like him, only understands the danger once he is on the rock. The pace then slows: a whole paragraph is spent standing still in fear, a structural pause that makes the reader share the paralysis. Alan\'s leap in the fifth paragraph removes the narrator\'s support, and the sixth opens with the isolating "I was now alone upon the rock". The climax is held back until the final sentence, where the hands slip twice before Alan drags him "into safety": the structure withholds relief until the last possible moment.',
  q4Focus: 'from "So there we stood" to the end.',
  q4Statement:
    'The writer makes the natural world feel genuinely dangerous and alive, creating real tension despite the reader knowing the narrator must survive because he is telling the story.',
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    'I agree. The water is described as "raging by" and the river as "dinning upon all sides", which makes it seem alive and angry. The narrator is so frightened that he covers his eyes twice. Even though we know he survives, the moment when his hands "slipped, caught again, slipped again" is tense because we do not know how he will be saved. Alan grabbing him "first by the hair" shows how close he came to falling.',
  q4Answer67:
    'I agree, and Stevenson meets the problem of the surviving narrator head on. The water is given the energy of a living thing: it is "raging by", the river is "dinning upon all sides", and the mist is left "hanging in the air". Yet the tension does not depend on whether David survives but on how. His fear is told with a frankness that an older narrator can afford: "a deadly sickness of fear", and a leap made "with that kind of anger of despair that has sometimes stood me in stead of courage". That last phrase reminds us that he is looking back, but it deepens the tension rather than removing it, because it admits that he was not brave. The final sentence delays rescue through a sequence of failures - "these slipped, caught again, slipped again" - until he is "sliddering back into the lynn", so the reader experiences the fall before the rescue. The narrator must survive, but the extract makes us feel that he very nearly did not, and that his survival was Alan\'s doing rather than his own.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    'Imagine a photograph of a rain-swollen river crashing through a dark, rocky gorge at night, barely visible in the darkness.',
  q5NarrativePrompt:
    'Write the opening part of a story about a character who must navigate a dangerous situation using only their wits.',
})

const p1_15 = makeP1(15, {
  set: '15',
  source:
    "Original composition in the style of Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie's Purple Hibiscus (in-copyright; do not cite as Adichie)",
  extract: `Papa sat at the head of the table, his Bible open beside his plate. The dining room was silent except for the clink of cutlery and the ticking of the grandfather clock in the hallway, which seemed to grow louder with each second, as though it were counting down to something. Mama served the rice quietly, her movements small and careful, the way she always moved in Papa's presence - as if she were handling something that might break, though the thing she was handling was herself.

Jaja sat opposite me. He was staring at his plate with the fixed concentration of someone trying very hard not to look anywhere else. His jaw was tight. I watched the small muscle at its hinge flex and release, flex and release, and I knew he was angry, though anger was not a word we used in our house. There were no words for certain things in our house. They existed, but we had not been given permission to name them.

"We will say grace," Papa said. It was not a suggestion.

We bowed our heads. Papa's voice filled the room - deep, measured, certain - and I tried to concentrate on the words, but instead I found myself looking at Mama's hands, which were pressed together so tightly that the knuckles had turned white. I noticed a bruise on her left wrist, just visible beneath the sleeve of her blouse, and I looked away quickly, the way you look away from the sun - not because you have seen enough, but because you cannot bear to see more.

The prayer ended. We ate. The clock ticked. And the silence sat among us like a fifth person at the table, eating nothing, saying everything.`,
  q1TrueStatements: 'up to "the thing she was handling was herself."',
  q1Options:
    'A) The family were eating in the kitchen.\nB) Papa was reading aloud from the Bible.\nC) The room was noisy.\nD) There was a clock nearby.\nE) Mama moved carefully.\nF) The grandfather clock was in the dining room.\nG) Papa sat at the head of the table.\nH) Mama was serving food.',
  q1Answers45:
    'D, E, G, H - D: "the ticking of the grandfather clock." E: "her movements small and careful." G: "Papa sat at the head of the table." H: "Mama served the rice quietly."',
  q1Answers67:
    'D, E, G, H - A is false: they were in "The dining room". B is false: Papa\'s Bible lay "open beside his plate", and the room "was silent". C is false: "The dining room was silent". F is false: the clock was "in the hallway".',
  q2Focus: 'from "We bowed our heads" to the end.',
  q2Text: 'to convey the atmosphere of fear and control within the family?',
  q2Answer45:
    'Mama\'s hands are "pressed together so tightly that the knuckles had turned white," showing her tension and fear. The bruise on her wrist suggests physical abuse. The simile comparing looking away to looking away from the sun is powerful - the narrator cannot bear to see the truth. The final personification of silence as "a fifth person at the table, eating nothing, saying everything" shows how much is being hidden.',
  q2Answer67:
    'Adichie constructs fear through the semiotics of the body - physical gestures that communicate what speech cannot. Mama\'s white knuckles during prayer transform a gesture of devotion into one of endurance; the religious act becomes a performance of submission. The bruise is disclosed and immediately retracted - "I looked away quickly" - the narrator\'s aversion enacting the family\'s systemic avoidance. The simile of the sun is precisely calibrated: it reframes looking away not as cowardice but as self-preservation, an inability to process rather than an unwillingness to see. The final personification - silence as "a fifth person" who says "everything" - is an oxymoronic climax: silence becomes the most eloquent speaker, and the paragraph\'s brevity - "The prayer ended. We ate. The clock ticked." - enacts that silence rhythmically, each short sentence a suppressed utterance.',
  q3Text: 'How has the writer structured the text to reveal the dynamics of this family?',
  q3Answer45:
    "The extract begins with Papa's authority - his position at the head of the table, his Bible. It then shifts to each family member in turn: Mama's careful movements, Jaja's anger, the narrator's observation. The command \"We will say grace\" shows Papa controls everything. The structure reveals the family through what they do not say - the silence, the suppressed emotions, the bruise quickly looked away from.",
  q3Answer67:
    "Adichie structures the extract as a series of perspectives rotating around the fixed centre of Papa's authority. Paragraph one establishes the spatial and power dynamics: Papa at the head, Bible as symbol of moral authority, Mama's constrained movement. The shift to Jaja in paragraph two introduces suppressed resistance - the flexing jaw is a body that speaks what the mouth cannot. The dialogue in paragraph three - a single imperative sentence - is structurally isolated, its brevity performing the absoluteness of Papa's control. The prayer scene in paragraph four shifts to the narrator's observing consciousness, and the bruise functions as the extract's structural revelation - the physical evidence of what the family's silence conceals. The final paragraph's staccato sentences mirror the opening's silence but now the reader understands what fills it. The personification of silence as a fifth presence transforms absence into the text's most significant character.",
  q4Focus: 'from "We bowed our heads."',
  q4Statement:
    "The writer is brilliant at showing domestic abuse not through dramatic scenes of violence, but through the tiny details of family life - the way people move, what they look at, what they don't say.",
  q4Text: '',
  q4Answer45:
    "I agree. The writer never describes violence directly but we understand it through small details: Mama's careful movements, the bruise half-hidden by a sleeve, the narrator looking away. Jaja's tight jaw shows anger he cannot express. These quiet details feel more real and more disturbing than dramatic violence because they show how abuse becomes normal family life.",
  q4Answer67:
    'I agree strongly. Adichie\'s technique is one of strategic indirection - the abuse is never narrated but is everywhere inferred. The bruise on Mama\'s wrist is the only piece of direct evidence, and even this is immediately suppressed by the narrator\'s averted gaze. The true violence is structural: it exists in the constrained movements ("small and careful"), the unnamed emotions ("anger was not a word we used"), the compulsory silence. The statement "We will say grace" - not a suggestion - encodes the family\'s entire power dynamic in six words. What makes this approach devastating is that it replicates the psychology of abuse itself: the family has learned to see and not-see simultaneously, to register harm and immediately look away. The reader is drawn into this complicity, recognising the bruise as the narrator does and sharing the impulse to look away. Adichie makes domestic abuse legible not through its exceptional moments but through its quotidian texture.',
  q5ImageDesc:
    'Imagine a photograph of a formal dining table set for a family meal, with a large Bible open at one end and four place settings in dim, golden light.',
  q5NarrativePrompt:
    'Write the opening part of a story about a family gathering where something important remains unspoken.',
})

